---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';

const path = '/apps/postscript';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'apps/postscript');
const desc = description(site, 'apps/postscript');

---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/apps">← All app reviews</a>
    <p class="badge mt-6">Supplier guide · checked November 25, 2026</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Postscript for Shopify: SMS operations and stack fit</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">Evaluate Postscript when cart recovery, launch alerts and customer replies justify a dedicated messaging operation. This is a supplier guide and a proposed acceptance plan; it contains no observed merchant tests, ratings or measured performance ranking.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>What is currently offered</h2>
      <p>Postscript’s <a class="link" href="https://postscript.io/pricing">pricing page</a> lists SMS/RCS plans, Shopify-data segmentation, automation templates and subscriber response management. It also advertises email with a waitlist. Ask which channels are available to your account before planning a consolidated stack; neither an SMS-only description nor a promise of immediate email replacement is reliable.</p>
      <p>If you already run email elsewhere, decide which system owns each recovery step. A second sender adds value only if it supplies a needed capability or incremental contribution after fees and staff time. Existing email automation is a reason to test coexistence carefully, rather than to migrate it by default.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Three acceptance exercises for different store needs</h2>
      <p><strong>Streetwear restock:</strong> use an illustrative $210 sneaker cart to check what happens when stock disappears between enrollment and dispatch. Trace the recovery link to the correct variant, replay the event, then place an order before the next step. Accept the flow only when duplicate events and completed purchases do not leave stale recovery messages queued. Choose the delay from the stock window rather than copying a universal two-hour schedule.</p>
      <p><strong>Denim sizing replies:</strong> send a test question asking for 32x34 availability. Confirm who receives it, what inventory information that person can see, and how an unanswered conversation escalates. Response management is listed; automatic live-stock answers and a particular support-time saving are not established here. Include staffing during a sale in the buying decision.</p>
      <p><strong>Supplement replenishment:</strong> distinguish a requested back-in-stock alert from recurring promotional reminders. Map active subscriptions, cancellations and stock notices to their actual purposes; subscription status alone does not decide all eligibility. Keep educational email and urgent stock alerts under named owners so an order cancels obsolete reminders across both queues.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Budget by segments and contribution</h2>
      <p>The linked price schedule lists a Starter minimum spend and separate message and carrier charges. Obtain a quote for your destination mix, SMS segments, MMS, sender type and optional services. A photograph, longer copy or a peak sale audience changes the workload; a contact count alone does not price a campaign.</p>
      <p><strong>Illustrative economics, not a vendor quote:</strong> at $65 order value, $25 variable product/fulfillment cost and $5 discount, contribution before messaging is $35 per incremental order. A hypothetical $280 total monthly messaging and operating cost needs 8 incremental orders to break even: 280 / (65 − 25 − 5) = 8. Attributed orders are not automatically incremental. Include returns and compare eligible randomized groups with uncertainty before scaling.</p>
      <p>Price a normal month and your planned peak separately. Include email-platform fees if retained, creative work, reply coverage and integration maintenance. Compare matched volumes against <a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-postscript">Klaviyo</a> or <a class="link" href="/compare/omnisend-vs-postscript">Omnisend</a>; this page establishes no lower-cost winner.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Cutover and ongoing ownership</h2>
      <p>Before import, retain channel/purpose permission evidence and withdrawal history. Reconcile suppression counts, sender readiness and queued recovery steps. Test opt-out ingestion and cancellation at dispatch, including purchases arriving from another channel. Treat vendor controls as controls to verify, not legal clearance.</p>
      <p>For a proposed VIP launch, validate the source segment and refresh timing before enrollment. A loyalty tag is neither SMS permission nor proof of current eligibility. Give the campaign, automation queue and reply inbox named owners; document a pause procedure when data or stock is wrong.</p>
      <p>Keep Postscript on the shortlist when these SMS workflows matter enough to operate and fund them. Defer expansion when there is no reply owner or no evidence of contribution beyond existing recovery. There is no universal order-volume threshold. See the <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">cart recovery guide</a> for planning context.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
